       following command line options:




       --mmaappffiillee==_p_a_l_e_t_t_e_f_i_l_e_n_a_m_e
              This names the file that contains the palette image.

              This option is mandatory.


       --ffllooyydd

       --ffss

       --nnooffllooyydd

       --nnooffss  These options determine whether  ppnnmmrreemmaapp  does  Floyd-Steinberg
              dithering.  Without Floyd-Steinberg, ppnnmmrreemmaapp selects the output
              color of a pixel based on the color of  only  the  corresponding
              input  pixel.   With Floyd-Steinberg, ppnnmmrreemmaapp considers regions
              of pixels such that the average color of a region is the same in
              the  output  as in the input.  The dithering effect appears as a
              dot pattern up close, but from a distance,  the  dots  blend  so
              that you see more colors than are present in the color map.

              As  an example, if your color map contains only black and white,
              and the input image has 4 adjacent pixels of gray, ppnnmmrreemmaapp with
              Floyd-Steinberg  would  generate  output  pixels  black,  white,
              black, white, which from a distance  looks  gray.   But  without
              Floyd-Steinberg,  ppnnmmrreemmaapp  would generate 4 white pixels, white
              being the single-pixel approximation of gray.

              Floyd-Steinberg gives vastly better results on images where  un‐
              modified quantization has banding or other artifacts, especially
              when going to a small number of colors such  as  the  above  IBM
              set.  However, it does take substantially more CPU time.

              --ffss is a synonym for --ffllooyydd.  --nnooffss is a synonym for --nnooffllooyydd.

              The default is --nnooffllooyydd.

              Before  Netpbm  10.46 (March 2009), dithering doesn't work quite
              as you expect if the color map has a lower maxval than  the  in‐
              put.   ppnnmmrreemmaapp  reduces the color resolution to the color map's
              maxval before doing any dithering, so  the  dithering  does  not
              have  the  effect  of making the image, at a distance, appear to
              have the original maxval.  In current Netpbm, it does.


       --nnoorraannddoomm
              This option affects a detail of  the  Floyd-Steinberg  dithering
              process.   It  has no effect if you aren't doing Floyd-Steinberg
              dithering.

              By default, ppnnmmrreemmaapp initializes the error propagation accumula‐
              tor  to  random  values to avoid the appearance of unwanted pat‐
              terns.  This is an extension of the original Floyd-Steinberg al‐
              gorithm.

              A  drawback  of this is that the same ppnnmmrreemmaapp on the same input
              produces slightly different output every time, which makes  com‐
              parison difficult.

              With  --nnoorraannddoomm,  ppnnmmrreemmaapp initializes the error accumulators to
              zero and the output is completely predictable.

              Alternatively, you can  use  --rraannddoommsseeeedd  to  get  randomization
              across the image, but still have repeatable results.

              You cannot specify this along with --rraannddoommsseeeedd.

              --nnoorraannddoomm was new in Netpbm 10.39 (June 2007).



       --rraannddoommsseeeedd==_n
              This  option  affects  a detail of the Floyd-Steinberg dithering
              process.  It has no effect if you aren't  doing  Floyd-Steinberg
              dithering.

              This  option  supplies  the seed for the random number generator
              used in the randomization process described in  the  explanation
              of  the  --nnoorraannddoomm  option.   If you run ppnnmmrreemmaapp twice with the
              same --rraannddoommsseeeedd value, you will get identical results.

              If you do not specify --rraannddoommsseeeedd, ppnnmmrreemmaapp chooses  a  seed  at
              random, adding another level of randomness to the dithering.

              You cannot specify this along with --nnoorraannddoomm.

              This option was new in Netpbm 10.82 (March 2018).



       --ffiirrssttiissddeeffaauulltt
              This  tells  ppnnmmrreemmaapp  to map any input color that is not in the
              palette to the first color in the  palette  (the  color  of  the
              pixel in the top left corner of the palette image)

              See DESCRIPTION ⟨#description⟩ .

              If  you  specify  --ffiirrssttiissddeeffaauulltt, the maxval of your input must
              match the maxval of your palette image.


       --mmiissssiinnggccoolloorr==_c_o_l_o_r_s_p_e_c
              This specifies the default color for ppnnmmrreemmaapp to map to a  color
              in  the input image that isn't in the palette.  _c_o_l_o_r may or may
              not be in the palette image; it is part of the  palette  regard‐
              less.

              _c_o_l_o_r_s_p_e_c   is   as   described   for   the   argument   of  the
              ppnnmm__ppaarrsseeccoolloorr(())                 library                 routine
              ⟨libnetpbm_image.html#colorname⟩ .

              If  you  specify  --mmiissssiinnggccoolloorr,  the  maxval of your input must
              match the maxval of your palette image.


       --vveerrbboossee
